1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:01,723
Hello, everyone.

2
00:00:03,223 --> 00:00:05,497
Let me start by asking you a question.

3
00:00:06,419 --> 00:00:11,457
If your production environment went
down right now, how many places

4
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:16,267
would your team have to search
before they could, confidently answer

5
00:00:16,353 --> 00:00:18,403
what happened and what to do next?

6
00:00:19,539 --> 00:00:22,720
The answer for many
enterprise is not one system.

7
00:00:23,938 --> 00:00:28,945
it can be multiple, like Jira,
ServiceNow, GitHub, Confluence,

8
00:00:30,284 --> 00:00:32,075
security platform, et cetera.

9
00:00:32,144 --> 00:00:34,870
Because there are a lot
of tribal knowledges that

10
00:00:34,870 --> 00:00:36,632
spread across multiple teams.

11
00:00:37,644 --> 00:00:43,197
We have spent years building systems
to capture enterprise knowledge, but

12
00:00:43,197 --> 00:00:47,836
we haven't necessarily solved the
problem of connecting that knowledge

13
00:00:47,836 --> 00:00:52,786
securely and retrieving the right piece
of information at the right moment.

14
00:00:53,907 --> 00:00:57,745
And now we have adding
gen AI to this equation.

15
00:00:59,158 --> 00:01:05,092
That creates an enormous opportunity,
but also a new risk because an AI system

16
00:01:05,093 --> 00:01:10,186
that retrieves the wrong information,
outdated information, or information

17
00:01:10,195 --> 00:01:14,755
that user isn't authorized to see,
can produce a very convincingly ans--

18
00:01:14,783 --> 00:01:16,436
convincing answer that is still wrong.

19
00:01:17,664 --> 00:01:21,769
So Enterprise RAG isn't really a
chatbot problem, it's a knowledge

20
00:01:21,770 --> 00:01:23,593
security and trust problem.

21
00:01:24,761 --> 00:01:29,339
That's what I did like to explore
today, how we move from a basic

22
00:01:29,339 --> 00:01:32,619
RAG implementation to a secure
enterprise knowledge system

23
00:01:33,087 --> 00:01:35,367
that people can actually trust.

24
00:01:36,543 --> 00:01:42,394
And that sta-starts with understanding
the problem we are trying to solve.

25
00:01:43,735 --> 00:01:46,945
For those who I haven't met,
my name is Ananda Kumar De.

26
00:01:47,277 --> 00:01:50,553
I'm a technology and AI leader
with two decades of experience

27
00:01:50,555 --> 00:01:51,810
in enterprise technology.

28
00:01:52,393 --> 00:01:57,455
And today, I lead AI strategy and solution
architect at Quadrant Technologies.

29
00:01:58,473 --> 00:02:02,268
a significant part of my work
is focused on taking AI from

30
00:02:02,730 --> 00:02:07,466
experimentation into practical,
secure enterprise scalable solutions

31
00:02:08,466 --> 00:02:12,251
So let's try to deep dive
into the problem a bit more.

32
00:02:13,176 --> 00:02:17,616
This is the reality behind
that opening question.

33
00:02:18,420 --> 00:02:24,183
The knowledge already exists, but it
is fragmented across systems and teams.

34
00:02:25,182 --> 00:02:31,239
And when we rely primarily on keyword
search, we often miss the context needed

35
00:02:31,246 --> 00:02:33,853
to answer a technical question correctly.

36
00:02:35,258 --> 00:02:41,018
In a production or security scenario,
that creates more than inconvenience.

37
00:02:42,033 --> 00:02:45,418
It can slow incident response
and affect reliability.

38
00:02:46,432 --> 00:02:52,498
So the first problem we need
to solve is not the LLM, it

39
00:02:52,512 --> 00:02:58,150
is making enterprise knowledge
accessible, relevant, and connected.

40
00:02:59,733 --> 00:03:04,001
That brings us to the architecture
that allows us to do that.

41
00:03:05,387 --> 00:03:10,859
So before we go into the details,
let's understand the topics that

42
00:03:10,859 --> 00:03:12,329
we are going to touch upon today.

43
00:03:13,500 --> 00:03:17,079
The RAG-- We will touch upon the RAG
fundamental architecture, the security

44
00:03:17,097 --> 00:03:23,117
governance, integration, grounded
retrieval and e-elevation, and advanced

45
00:03:23,117 --> 00:03:26,712
pattern and production lessons that
will help us understand how it can be

46
00:03:26,713 --> 00:03:28,457
used in a production level environment.

47
00:03:29,734 --> 00:03:33,675
So we'll, so we will start with first
the RAG architecture fundamentals.

48
00:03:35,366 --> 00:03:41,527
So let's come and try to look into
this architecture, and the-- all

49
00:03:41,527 --> 00:03:45,675
these components are pretty common to
most of the enterprise organization.

50
00:03:46,529 --> 00:03:53,479
At its core, the RAG, the Retrieval
Augmented, Retrieval-Augmented Generation

51
00:03:53,630 --> 00:03:58,676
architecture, creates a controlled path
between enterprise knowledge and the LLM.

52
00:03:59,754 --> 00:04:04,037
The user asks a question, we process
the query, retrieves relevant

53
00:04:04,038 --> 00:04:09,002
enterprise context, augment the
prompt, and then generate the response.

54
00:04:09,635 --> 00:04:14,113
But notice that the LLM is only
one part of the architecture.

55
00:04:15,372 --> 00:04:18,306
The real enterprise
complexity sits around it.

56
00:04:19,208 --> 00:04:26,102
Data ingestion, retrieval, access
control, observability, and grounding.

57
00:04:26,981 --> 00:04:33,287
That is what turns a simple GenAI
application into an enterprise RAG system

58
00:04:34,287 --> 00:04:40,624
And importantly, governance doesn't
begin with-- begin when the user submits

59
00:04:40,624 --> 00:04:43,844
a query, it start- starts much earlier

60
00:04:44,844 --> 00:04:48,884
So the first place we can introduce
control is during ingestion.

61
00:04:50,030 --> 00:04:56,204
We connect to systems such as GitHub,
Jira, Confluence, ServiceNow, and

62
00:04:56,204 --> 00:04:59,375
internal knowledge sys-- sources
through governed pipelines.

63
00:05:00,231 --> 00:05:05,543
Then we have to make some important
decisions around chunking and metadata.

64
00:05:07,375 --> 00:05:08,917
Who owns the information?

65
00:05:09,402 --> 00:05:10,749
How sensitive is it?

66
00:05:11,586 --> 00:05:13,007
Where did it come from?

67
00:05:13,580 --> 00:05:19,348
And before we create embedding, we should
detect things like PII and secrets.

68
00:05:19,966 --> 00:05:23,567
That has been a very important
part of any organization.

69
00:05:25,461 --> 00:05:31,231
If we put bad or sensitive data into the
knowledge layer, retrieval, retrieval

70
00:05:31,305 --> 00:05:37,469
simplify-- simply amplifies the problem
Once the data is prepared properly, we

71
00:05:37,469 --> 00:05:40,288
can focus on the retrieval foundation

72
00:05:41,288 --> 00:05:45,918
So retrieval quality is influenced
by three fundamental choices.

73
00:05:46,610 --> 00:05:51,757
First is the embedding model,
how effectively we represent the

74
00:05:51,830 --> 00:05:53,995
meaning of technical content.

75
00:05:54,637 --> 00:05:59,066
Second is the vector store
and its ability to support

76
00:05:59,072 --> 00:06:00,992
filtering and enterprise control.

77
00:06:01,861 --> 00:06:05,593
And third, most important,
is index hygiene.

78
00:06:06,458 --> 00:06:10,511
In production, documents
change, permission change,

79
00:06:10,596 --> 00:06:12,279
and information becomes stale.

80
00:06:12,767 --> 00:06:18,388
So we need versioning, deletion
propagation, and lifecycle management.

81
00:06:19,784 --> 00:06:25,786
A production RAG system needs the same
operational discipline we apply to

82
00:06:25,811 --> 00:06:27,544
any other enterprise data platform.

83
00:06:29,413 --> 00:06:34,374
That leads to an important architectural
decision, where should we actually

84
00:06:34,381 --> 00:06:36,612
store and search these vectors?

85
00:06:37,612 --> 00:06:43,745
So these are several viable options,
and the right answer depends heavily

86
00:06:43,746 --> 00:06:45,471
on the enterprise environment.

87
00:06:46,934 --> 00:06:53,910
Pinecone, pgvector, you know,
Weaviate, ANSS, AI Search, Azure AI

88
00:06:53,917 --> 00:06:55,522
Search all have different strengths.

89
00:06:56,392 --> 00:07:02,412
Rather than asking which one has the
fastest benchmark, I would look at the

90
00:07:02,585 --> 00:07:08,775
complete operating model, hybrid search,
metadata filtering, security integration,

91
00:07:09,028 --> 00:07:13,987
scalability, and what infrastructure
the organization already operates.

92
00:07:15,699 --> 00:07:19,968
So if you are using into the-- if
you are into the Azure ecosystem, you

93
00:07:19,971 --> 00:07:22,303
can go ahead and use Azure AI Search.

94
00:07:22,671 --> 00:07:28,132
If you are okay to use open source,
vector stores, you can go for Pinecone.

95
00:07:29,552 --> 00:07:32,947
The vector database is an
architectural decision, not

96
00:07:32,959 --> 00:07:34,999
simply a performance decision.

97
00:07:36,080 --> 00:07:41,247
And regardless of which vector store we
choose, there is another important lesson.

98
00:07:41,966 --> 00:07:46,450
Semantic search alone isn't
enough for technical knowledge

99
00:07:47,450 --> 00:07:53,700
Considering a DevOps question
containing something like a CV number,

100
00:07:53,967 --> 00:07:59,678
an incident ID, or a configuration
key, semantic search is excellent in

101
00:07:59,684 --> 00:08:02,392
understanding concepts and paraphrase.

102
00:08:03,768 --> 00:08:08,626
But it may not recognize the
importance of an exact identifier.

103
00:08:10,108 --> 00:08:15,465
That's where sparse retrieval,
such as BM25 comes valuable.

104
00:08:16,358 --> 00:08:21,811
By combining dense and sparse retrieval
and then merging their ranking, we

105
00:08:21,811 --> 00:08:27,542
get the best of both worlds, semantic
understanding plus exact match precision.

106
00:08:29,386 --> 00:08:34,804
But even after hybrid retrieval,
we may still have more candidates

107
00:08:34,914 --> 00:08:40,996
than we want to send to the LLM
This is what re-ranking become…

108
00:08:41,010 --> 00:08:43,075
This is where re-ranking becomes useful.

109
00:08:44,070 --> 00:08:48,371
Instead of trying to identify the
per-perfect answer immediately,

110
00:08:49,124 --> 00:08:54,199
we first retrieve a broader set
of candidates, say the top fifty.

111
00:08:55,613 --> 00:09:02,372
A cross encoder then evaluates the query
and each candidate together and determines

112
00:09:02,828 --> 00:09:05,040
which chunks are actually most relevant.

113
00:09:06,407 --> 00:09:09,477
We can then pass only
the top few to the LLM.

114
00:09:10,172 --> 00:09:14,561
The important principle is
retrieve broadly, re-rank

115
00:09:14,719 --> 00:09:17,805
intelligently, and generate narrowly.

116
00:09:19,428 --> 00:09:23,593
That improves relevance while
controlling the prompt size and cost.

117
00:09:25,063 --> 00:09:30,116
But improving relevance introduces
a-another question that is even

118
00:09:30,117 --> 00:09:31,854
more important in an enterprise.

119
00:09:32,711 --> 00:09:37,282
Is the user actually allowed to
see the information we retrieve?

120
00:09:39,129 --> 00:09:42,494
So that is where we come
to security by design.

121
00:09:44,099 --> 00:09:45,595
It cannot be an option.

122
00:09:46,058 --> 00:09:51,565
It has to be by design while we build the
system This is one of the most important

123
00:09:51,570 --> 00:09:53,597
security principles in enterprise RAG.

124
00:09:54,448 --> 00:09:57,270
Authorization cannot simply happen at UI.

125
00:09:57,943 --> 00:10:01,317
The user's identity and
applicable policies need to

126
00:10:01,443 --> 00:10:03,436
influence retrieval itself.

127
00:10:04,765 --> 00:10:07,982
The policy engine evaluates
the request and metadata.

128
00:10:08,549 --> 00:10:13,173
Filters ensure that unauthorized
chunks are excluded before

129
00:10:13,198 --> 00:10:14,975
they enter the ranking process.

130
00:10:15,676 --> 00:10:19,821
That means the system doesn't
retrieve sensitive information and

131
00:10:19,823 --> 00:10:21,544
then try to hide it afterwards.

132
00:10:22,555 --> 00:10:25,600
Security becomes part of the
retrieval pipeline itself.

133
00:10:27,044 --> 00:10:33,338
And once we have that security boundary,
we also need to protect and audit

134
00:10:33,355 --> 00:10:38,215
the knowledge we are working with So
there are several-- there are really

135
00:10:38,251 --> 00:10:42,664
two responsibi- responsibilities
here, protecting the corpus and

136
00:10:42,676 --> 00:10:44,640
being able to prove what happened.

137
00:10:45,633 --> 00:10:50,451
Protection includes encryption,
secret scanning, classification, and

138
00:10:50,513 --> 00:10:52,888
appropriate retention and deletion.

139
00:10:53,694 --> 00:10:55,763
But we also need an audit trail.

140
00:10:56,601 --> 00:10:58,460
Who asked the ques- questions?

141
00:10:58,754 --> 00:11:00,046
What was retrieved?

142
00:11:00,862 --> 00:11:02,823
What response was generated?

143
00:11:03,349 --> 00:11:06,813
And which sources supported that response?

144
00:11:07,883 --> 00:11:12,376
That becomes particularly important
for security investigation

145
00:11:12,775 --> 00:11:14,250
and regulated environments.

146
00:11:15,576 --> 00:11:20,984
Trust requires not only
control, but evidence that those

147
00:11:21,010 --> 00:11:23,366
controls were actually applied.

148
00:11:24,819 --> 00:11:31,565
Security protects the system, but how do
we make sure the trust-- that makes sure

149
00:11:31,568 --> 00:11:34,167
the user trusts the answer themselves?

150
00:11:35,167 --> 00:11:37,895
Grounding retrieval build trust.

151
00:11:38,612 --> 00:11:44,426
So the responses should be grounded
and the LLM shouldn't hallucinate.

152
00:11:46,215 --> 00:11:48,931
Grounding is the next part
of that trust equation.

153
00:11:49,679 --> 00:11:55,052
We don't want the model drawing
from anything and everything.

154
00:11:55,467 --> 00:11:56,329
That is hallucination.

155
00:11:57,438 --> 00:12:03,386
We want authoritative re- enterprise
sources, and we want the generated

156
00:12:03,392 --> 00:12:07,382
response tied back to the evidence
retrieved from the sources.

157
00:12:08,386 --> 00:12:13,539
And when confidence is low or
the question is high risk, we

158
00:12:13,539 --> 00:12:15,511
can introduce human review.

159
00:12:16,300 --> 00:12:18,263
That is what we call as human in the loop.

160
00:12:19,114 --> 00:12:25,139
So instead of asking how do we eliminate
hallucination, the more practical

161
00:12:25,148 --> 00:12:31,207
enterprise question is: How do we design
controls that make unsupported answers

162
00:12:31,337 --> 00:12:33,974
difficult to produce and easy to detect?

163
00:12:34,974 --> 00:12:39,285
And once we have introduced
these controls, we need a way to

164
00:12:39,290 --> 00:12:45,592
demonstrate that these actually
work or is working This is where

165
00:12:45,596 --> 00:12:51,758
I recommend separating retrieval
equations from generation evaluations.

166
00:12:52,868 --> 00:12:57,715
Before answering or before asking
whether the LLM produces a good

167
00:12:58,251 --> 00:13:02,720
question, let's ask whether we
retrieve the right evidence.

168
00:13:03,936 --> 00:13:08,129
Precision and recall tells
us about retrieval quality.

169
00:13:09,104 --> 00:13:17,242
MRR tells us where the retrieval results
appearing-- appears in the ranking, and

170
00:13:17,242 --> 00:13:23,103
the RAGs gives us additional measures
around faithfulness and answer relevance.

171
00:13:24,587 --> 00:13:29,750
The important point is to establish
a baseline with representative

172
00:13:29,825 --> 00:13:34,185
enterprise question and then
optimize against evidence.

173
00:13:35,467 --> 00:13:38,637
Don't tune RAG by intuition, measure it.

174
00:13:40,534 --> 00:13:45,013
Once the fundamentals are working,
we can start addressing more complex

175
00:13:45,015 --> 00:13:50,168
enterprise questions That is where
we come to the advanced RAG patterns.

176
00:13:51,168 --> 00:13:55,907
Not every enterprise question
can be answered by finding the

177
00:13:55,911 --> 00:13:57,806
three most similar paragraphs.

178
00:13:58,968 --> 00:14:01,977
Some questions require
understanding relationships.

179
00:14:02,379 --> 00:14:06,414
For example, which application
depends on a particular service,

180
00:14:06,648 --> 00:14:11,399
who owns it, and whether there are
related incidents or vulnerabilities.

181
00:14:12,555 --> 00:14:15,442
That's where graph
retrieval becomes useful.

182
00:14:16,472 --> 00:14:22,036
Agentic RAG can then decompose, complex
questions into multiple retrieval steps,

183
00:14:22,955 --> 00:14:29,984
while tool-augmented RAG can bring it
live information from enterprise APIs.

184
00:14:31,062 --> 00:14:35,818
The architecture becomes
progressively more sophisticated as

185
00:14:35,818 --> 00:14:37,780
the questions become more complex.

186
00:14:38,911 --> 00:14:43,305
But sophistication doesn't automatically
make a RAG system successful.

187
00:14:44,277 --> 00:14:48,953
Production experience points
us back to a few fundamentals

188
00:14:49,953 --> 00:14:54,065
These are four lessons I would
emphasize from a production perspective.

189
00:14:55,348 --> 00:14:58,751
First, fix the data
before tuning the model.

190
00:15:00,072 --> 00:15:04,650
Better prompts can't compensate
for poor source data.

191
00:15:05,603 --> 00:15:09,201
Second, enforce authorization
inside retrieval.

192
00:15:10,785 --> 00:15:17,381
Third, establish measurements before
changing models, chunking or prompts.

193
00:15:18,136 --> 00:15:25,217
And finally, operate RAG like software,
version it, monitor it, test it, deploy

194
00:15:25,219 --> 00:15:29,792
it through control pipeline before
having an incident response process.

195
00:15:31,001 --> 00:15:35,627
RAG is not a prompt engineering
project, it's a production system.

196
00:15:37,147 --> 00:15:40,833
I will repeat once again, RAG
is not a prompt engineering

197
00:15:40,835 --> 00:15:43,305
project, it's a production system.

198
00:15:44,305 --> 00:15:48,319
So if we bring everything
together, a trusted enterprise RAG

199
00:15:48,323 --> 00:15:50,022
system rests on four principles.

200
00:15:51,309 --> 00:15:56,586
Secure the pipeline, use hybrid
retrieval, evaluate continuously,

201
00:15:57,222 --> 00:15:59,967
and design explicitly for trust.

202
00:16:01,512 --> 00:16:04,660
And you don't have to start
with a massive transformation.

203
00:16:04,944 --> 00:16:08,391
Start by auditing and classifying
your knowledge sources.

204
00:16:09,561 --> 00:16:15,421
Build a small golden dataset for
representative questions, then establish

205
00:16:15,435 --> 00:16:20,777
hybrid retrieval and authorization as
your baseline and measure the results.

206
00:16:21,743 --> 00:16:25,353
Start with the foundation,
prove the value, and then

207
00:16:25,356 --> 00:16:26,925
progressively add sophistication.

208
00:16:28,316 --> 00:16:34,807
If there is one thing I would leave you
with today is this, enterprise RAG is not

209
00:16:34,813 --> 00:16:37,012
about giving an LLM access to more data.

210
00:16:37,752 --> 00:16:41,708
It is about giving the right user access
to the right knowledge at the right time

211
00:16:41,768 --> 00:16:43,938
with enough evidence to trust the answer.

212
00:16:45,351 --> 00:16:49,595
That is the difference between an
AI demo and an enterprise-grade,

213
00:16:49,827 --> 00:16:55,116
grade AI system Thank you for
your time and being here with me

